{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Inductive Bias in Machine Learning.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Inductive Bias in Machine Learning.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"inductive bias in machine learning inductive bias in machine learning","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Inductive Bias in Machine Learning.md","fileName":"Inductive Bias in Machine Learning.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Inductive Bias in Machine Learning","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-15 02:18\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Machine Learning and Neural Networks]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Machine Learning and Neural Networks","Ill-Posed Learning Problem","Model Underfitting","Model Overfitting","deeplearning_mit.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Inductive Bias in Machine Learning.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Inductive Bias in Machine Learning.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"inductive bias in machine learning inductive bias in machine learning","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Inductive Bias in Machine Learning.md","fileName":"Inductive Bias in Machine Learning.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Inductive Bias in Machine Learning","heading":"Inductive Bias in Machine Learning","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Inductive bias is the set of assumptions a learning algorithm uses to prefer some functions over others beyond what the examples alone establish. A model restricted to straight-line relationships, for instance, assumes linear structure even when the dataset might also support more complex mappings.\n\nThe bias counterbalances the [[Ill-Posed Learning Problem|ambiguity of learning from limited examples]]. If it is too restrictive for the domain, the chosen function may [[Model Underfitting|underfit]]; if it is too permissive relative to the available data, it may [[Model Overfitting|overfit]] noise. Kelleher describes neural networks as having relatively weak bias, which helps them learn flexible relationships but also increases their demand for substantial data.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Machine Learning and Neural Networks","Ill-Posed Learning Problem","Model Underfitting","Model Overfitting","deeplearning_mit.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Inductive Bias in Machine Learning.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Inductive Bias in Machine Learning.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"inductive bias in machine learning inductive bias in machine learning","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Inductive Bias in Machine Learning.md","fileName":"Inductive Bias in Machine Learning.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Inductive Bias in Machine Learning","heading":"References","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"[[deeplearning_mit.epub]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Machine Learning and Neural Networks","Ill-Posed Learning Problem","Model Underfitting","Model Overfitting","deeplearning_mit.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""}]}